    "slug": "double-cleansing",
    "title": "Double cleansing",
    "summary": "Oil first, then water. When you need it and when you really don't.",
    "minutes": 4,
    "lede": "Double cleansing is washing your face twice in a row at night with two different kinds of cleanser. It sounds like extra work. It takes about a minute, and it is the reason Korean routines can wear sunscreen every single day without clogged pores.",
    "short": [
      "Oil or balm first, then your normal cleanser, at night only.",
      "Needed after sunscreen or makeup, which is most nights.",
      "Never kitchen oil, never in the morning, never hot water."
    ],
    "body": "<h2>What it is</h2>\n<p>Step one is an oil-based cleanser: a cleansing oil or a solid balm that melts on the skin. Oil dissolves oil, so it lifts off sunscreen, makeup, your own sebum and the sticky film of pollution, none of which rinse away well with water alone. Step two is your normal water-based cleanser, a gel, foam, cream or milk, which clears sweat, water-based grime and any oily residue. The result is skin that is clean without feeling tight.</p>\n<p>Wipes and micellar water on their own are a poor substitute. Wipes in particular tend to move things around rather than take them off.</p>\n\n<h2>Why Korean skincare treats it as normal</h2>\n<p>Korean routines are built on daily sunscreen and light hydrating layers. Sunscreen is designed to cling to skin, so an oil cleanser is the reliable way to remove it fully without scrubbing. UK acne guidance says the same thing from the other direction: take makeup off at the end of every day, because leaving residue overnight is a common cause of blocked pores.</p>\n\n<h2>When you need it</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>Evenings after wearing sunscreen, which should be most evenings.</li>\n  <li>Evenings after makeup, especially long-wear or waterproof.</li>\n  <li>After heavy sweating or a day in city traffic, or any day your skin feels greasy or gritty.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>When you don't</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>Mornings. Nothing went on your face overnight, so one gentle cleanse, or just lukewarm water if your skin is dry, is enough.</li>\n  <li>Evenings when you wore nothing at all, for example a day indoors.</li>\n  <li>Dry, normal or sensitive skin may not need it every night. Start a couple of times a week and see how your skin responds.</li>\n  <li>If you have eczema or rosacea, check with a GP or pharmacist before adding a cleansing step.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>How to do it</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li>Dry hands, dry face. Massage a pump of oil, or a pearl-sized scoop of balm, over the whole face in gentle circles for thirty to sixty seconds. Spend a little longer on the hairline, nose and jaw where sunscreen builds up. Keep it out of your eyes unless the label says it is eye-safe.</li>\n  <li>Wet your hands and keep massaging. A proper cleansing oil turns milky when it meets water (the label will call this emulsifying). That is what lets it rinse clean.</li>\n  <li>Rinse with lukewarm water.</li>\n  <li>Follow with your water-based cleanser, rinse, pat dry.</li>\n  <li>Carry on with toner and the rest of your night routine.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>Using kitchen oil. Olive or coconut oil does not emulsify, so it sits on the skin and can block pores. Use a product made to rinse off.</li>\n  <li>Skipping the second cleanse because the oil felt like enough. Leftover cleansing oil can itself cause congestion.</li>\n  <li>Double cleansing in the morning too. That is over-cleansing.</li>\n  <li>A harsh, high-foaming second cleanser that leaves skin squeaky. Match it to your skin type.</li>\n  <li>Hot water and scrubbing. Warm water and fingertips only.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>A note on heartleaf and other named oils</h2>\n<p>Cleansing oils are sold by their star plant: heartleaf, rice, green tea. They are all the same category of product, an oil or balm that emulsifies and rinses. The base does the cleaning; the plant is a bonus. Heartleaf (houttuynia cordata) is popular for oily, spot-prone and easily reddened skin because it is calming and lightly oil-controlling, which makes a heartleaf cleansing oil a sensible first pick for a lot of beginners. Choose by how it rinses and how your skin feels afterwards, not by the headline ingredient.</p>\n\n<h2>By skin type</h2>\n<p>Dry or sensitive: a fragrance-free oil or balm, then a creamy or milky second cleanser. Oily or spot-prone: a light oil labelled non-comedogenic (made to be less likely to block pores), then a gentle gel. Combination or normal: either works; a balm followed by a hydrating gel suits most people.</p>",
