    "slug": "myths",
    "title": "Myths, and a few red flags",
    "summary": "Natural is better, more steps is better, toothpaste on spots, and other things to unlearn.",
    "minutes": 4,
    "lede": "A lot of skincare advice is passed down like folklore. Here is the short list of things that are simply not true, and a few warning signs when you are shopping.",
    "short": [
      "Natural is not automatically gentler, and expensive is not automatically better.",
      "Pores do not shrink and toothpaste does not treat spots.",
      "You need sunscreen in the UK, on cloudy days, whatever your skin tone."
    ],
    "body": "<h2>Things that are not true</h2>\n<p><strong>Natural is always gentler.</strong> Lemon juice can burn and leave dark patches, especially in sunlight. Undiluted essential oils cause blistering and allergy. Judge an ingredient on evidence and on how your own skin reacts, not on where it came from.</p>\n<p><strong>More steps mean better skin.</strong> Stacking products raises the odds that ingredients clash and your skin gets overwhelmed. Three good steps done every day beat ten done badly.</p>\n<p><strong>The SPF in my makeup is enough.</strong> Nobody applies foundation thickly enough to reach the number on the label, it rarely covers the neck and ears, and some have no UVA protection. Proper sunscreen first; SPF makeup is a bonus.</p>\n<p><strong>This shrinks pores.</strong> Pores do not open, close or shrink. They look bigger when clogged, oily or sun-damaged, and smaller when kept clear and protected. Products can only make them less noticeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Drinking more water fixes dry skin.</strong> Unless you are seriously dehydrated, extra glasses of water do not plump skin. Moisturisers and humectants do that from the outside.</p>\n<p><strong>Expensive means better.</strong> Price reflects packaging and marketing as much as the formula. Ceramides, glycerin and hyaluronic acid are in plenty of cheap products, and fragrance, the most common unnecessary irritant, is in plenty of pricey ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Lemon, baking soda or vinegar make a cheap exfoliant.</strong> Lemon is strongly acidic and can cause burns and lasting discolouration. Baking soda is strongly alkaline and strips the barrier. Neither is controlled in strength the way a formulated product is.</p>\n<p><strong>Toothpaste dries out spots.</strong> The ingredient that once seemed to work has mostly been removed. What is left burns and irritates. Use a benzoyl peroxide or salicylic acid spot treatment, or a patch.</p>\n<p><strong>Spots mean you are dirty.</strong> The dark colour of a blackhead is oxidised oil and pigment, not dirt. Scrubbing harder makes acne worse.</p>\n<p><strong>Cutting out chocolate will clear acne.</strong> UK dermatology bodies say there is not enough evidence to recommend any specific diet for acne. Eat normally.</p>\n<p><strong>You don't need sunscreen in the UK, or when it is cloudy.</strong> Most UV passes through cloud, and UK sun is strong enough to damage skin from March to October.</p>\n<p><strong>Darker skin doesn't need SPF.</strong> Everyone can burn and get skin cancer, and darker skin pigments more easily after irritation, which sunscreen helps prevent.</p>\n<p><strong>Tingling means it is working.</strong> A brief tingle from vitamin C or an acid can be normal. Stinging that persists means the product is too strong or does not suit you.</p>\n<p><strong>Wait five minutes between layers.</strong> A few seconds of patting in is enough for most products.</p>\n\n<h2>Red flags when shopping</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>Promises to cure acne overnight, detox skin or permanently shrink pores.</li>\n  <li>Exotic ingredients with no research behind them.</li>\n  <li>Shops selling prescription-strength retinoids or hydroquinone without a prescription.</li>\n  <li>Suspiciously cheap sunscreen from marketplace sellers. Counterfeits and badly stored stock are real; buy from proper retailers and check the expiry date.</li>\n  <li>Any recommendation that earns the writer money without saying so. UK rules require a clear label.</li>\n  <li>Strong actives marketed to children and young teens. Under 16, the routine is cleanser, moisturiser and sunscreen unless a doctor says otherwise.</li>\n</ul>",
    "sources": [
      "https://slmdskincare.com/blogs/learn/5-diy-spot-treatments-to-avoid",
      "https://www.tirabeauty.com/article/articles/dr-monica-jacobs-beginner-friendly-guide-to-korean-skincare-ingredients",
      "https://health.clevelandclinic.org/is-the-sunscreen-in-your-makeup-enough",
      "https://www.aad.org/public/everyday-care/skin-care-secrets/face/treat-large-pores",
      "https://www.webmd.com/beauty/features/drink-water-skin",
      "https://newsroom.clevelandclinic.org/2026/04/14/why-expensive-beauty-products-are-not-always-better",
      "https://www.skinhealthinfo.org.uk/condition/acne/",
      "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/NG198/chapter/recommendations",
      "https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/causes-of-cancer/sun-uv-and-cancer/ways-to-enjoy-the-sun-safely",
      "https://www.britishskinfoundation.org.uk/sun-safety-with-dr-mary-sommerlad",
      "https://www.asa.org.uk/advice-online/influencers-guide.html",
      "https://www.paiskincare.com/blogs/news/teen-tween-active-skincare"
    ]
